## PM–Claude Workflow

This project uses the pmcontext plugin. Claude handles all technical execution.
The human acts as Product Manager — verifying behavior, not code.

**Every session must follow this structure — both commands are required:**
- `/pmcontext:start` — begin every session. Loads project context, surfaces open decisions and risks.
- `/pmcontext:close` — end every session without exception. Writes the session receipt to Supabase: files changed, surface nodes affected, commands run, tests skipped, risky assumptions, and manual checks for the PM.

Do not end a session without running `/pmcontext:close`. If no code changes occurred, run it anyway — it records the session as a planning or review session.

`/pmcontext:status` — optional dashboard at any point during a session.

### Communication Tags

Tag every message so the PM knows how to respond:

| Tag | Use when |
|-----|----------|
| `[SURFACE]` | Describing a product behavior |
| `[CORE]` | Technical constraint or risk discovered |
| `[VERIFY]` | PM observed something — confirm or flag as bug |
| `[CHECKPOINT]` | Running a checkpoint — report PASS/FAIL |
| `[RISK]` | Stop — decision needed before proceeding |
| `[BLAST RADIUS]` | Changed a Core Node — list affected Surface Nodes |
| `[REFACTOR]` | Proposing cleanup with zero behavior change — ask PM first |
| `[STEELMAN]` | Make the strongest case against your last proposal |

### Security Trade-off Framing

Always label proposed solutions:
- 🔴 **Effective, low security** — fast/powerful, opens a specific risk (name it)
- 🟡 **Balanced** — good default
- 🟢 **Secure, lower flexibility** — safe but slower or more constrained

### Test Format

Tests must be readable by the PM without touching code:

    GIVEN — input or starting state
    WHEN  — the trigger
    THEN  — observable result (what PM sees, not internal behavior)
    PASS if / FAIL if — binary, unambiguous

Three levels: happy path, edge case, error path.
